::PC_U02_L02_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
What is the end behavior of a cubic polynomial with positive leading coefficient?
{
=A. left down, right up#Correct. Odd degree with positive leading coefficient falls left and rises right.
~B. both ends up#Both ends up describes even degree with positive leading coefficient.
~C. both ends down#Both ends down describes even degree with negative leading coefficient.
~D. left up, right down#This describes odd degree with negative leading coefficient.
}
::PC_U02_L02_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
At a zero with even multiplicity, what does the graph usually do?
{
~A. crosses the x-axis#Crossing is associated with odd multiplicity.
=B. touches and turns#Correct. Even multiplicity usually means the graph touches and turns.
~C. has a vertical asymptote#Polynomial zeros do not create vertical asymptotes.
~D. has no intercept#A real zero is an x-intercept.
}
::PC_U02_L02_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which polynomial has degree 4?
{
~A. x^3+2x#The highest exponent is 3.
~B. 5x^2-1#The highest exponent is 2.
=C. (x-1)^2(x+3)^2#Correct. The exponents add to 4.
~D. 7x+4#This is degree 1.
}
::PC_U02_L02_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which key feature is found where a graph crosses the x-axis?
{
=A. x-intercept#Correct. A crossing on the x-axis is an x-intercept.
~B. y-intercept#A y-intercept occurs where x=0.
~C. range only#Range is an output set, not a crossing point.
~D. leading coefficient#The leading coefficient affects end behavior, not the specific crossing label.
}
::PC_U02_L02_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
How many turning points can a degree 5 polynomial have at most?
{
~A. 5#This confuses degree with maximum turning points.
~B. 6#This is greater than the maximum for degree 5.
~C. 1#Some degree 5 polynomials can have more than one turning point.
=D. 4#Correct. A degree n polynomial has at most n-1 turning points.
}